  "transcript": "SEAT: HOLD-APART (the Gap, xor). Instance: examples/field-guide, run r2/r2.1, proposal p2-restructure-lists-and-timing-table.\n\n=== STEP 1 - NUMBERED FACT LIST (from artifact/GUIDE.md body) ===\nNUMBERED FACT LIST (original artifact/GUIDE.md body, split on . ! ? in order; every sentence stating a checkable quantity/duration/temperature/item/instruction indexed F1 upward):\nF1  This guide explains how to prepare an ordinary household to manage safely and comfortably through a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours. [scope: up to 72 hours]\nF2  It is written for a temperate climate and assumes no backup generator. [temperate; no generator]\nF3  Work through the sections in order; the earlier sections cover the preparations that matter most. [work in order]\nF4  Store four litres of water per person per day, which means twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours. [4 L/person/day = 12 L/72h]\nF5  Half of that allowance is for drinking, and the other half covers cooking and basic hygiene. [half drink / half cook+hygiene]\nF6  Store the water in food-grade containers, keep the containers out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months. [food-grade; no sunlight; replace 6 months]\nF7  If a storm is forecast and you have advance warning, also fill the bathtub; bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never for drinking. [fill bathtub; flush/wash not drink]\nF8  Keep a dedicated shelf of non-perishable food sufficient for three days. [3 days]\nF9  Good choices are canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, and shelf-stable milk. [6 items]\nF10 Choose foods that require no cooking, and make sure you own a manual can opener, because an electric one will be useless. [no-cook; manual can opener]\nF11 Eat the perishable food from the refrigerator first, then the food from the freezer, and only then open the emergency shelf. [order: fridge, freezer, shelf]\nF12 An unopened refrigerator keeps food safe for about four hours. [~4 h fridge]\nF13 A full freezer holds a safe temperature for about forty-eight hours, but a half-full freezer holds it for only about twenty-four hours, so it is worth keeping the freezer consolidated and full, even if some of the space is taken up by containers of frozen water. [~48 h full / ~24 h half]\nF14 Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a note to each door as a reminder. [doors closed; note each door]\nF15 Any perishable food that has spent more than two hours above four degrees Celsius should be discarded; when in doubt, throw it out. [>2 h above 4 C discard]\nF16 Use battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as your primary light source, and avoid candles entirely, since candles are a leading cause of house fires during outages. [LED lanterns/headlamps; no candles]\nF17 Store at least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light. [1 light/person + 2 spare battery sets]\nF18 Keep one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, and place them where they can be found in the dark. [1 headlamp/bed; 1 lantern kitchen]\nF19 Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window, because each of these produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal. [no combustion indoors; CO odourless/fatal]\nF20 Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep. [>=1 CO alarm per sleeping floor]\nF21 In cold weather, choose one small room on a south-facing side of the home, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater. [one small south-facing room; layers/blankets]\nF22 If the indoor temperature falls below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot maintain warmth, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home. [below 10 C relocate]\nF23 Keep a battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts, and know the frequency of your local emergency station before the outage begins. [radio; know station frequency]\nF24 Charge every phone and power bank whenever a storm is forecast; a full modern power bank of twenty thousand milliamp-hours will recharge a typical phone roughly four times. [20,000 mAh ~4 recharges]\nF25 Put your phone in low-power mode at the start of the outage, and agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every member of the household will text, because text messages get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls. [low-power mode; one out-of-area text contact]\nF26 Keep a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, and rotate the medications so they stay in date. [first-aid kit; 7-day meds; rotate]\nF27 Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally remains usable at room temperature for up to twenty-eight days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist before an emergency happens. [insulin 28 days; confirm pharmacist]\nF28 Write down the phone numbers of your pharmacy, your doctor, and the poison-control line on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book. [pharmacy/doctor/poison-control on paper]\nF29 Wait five minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise. [wait 5 minutes]\nF30 Check the freezer: if food still contains ice crystals, it can be safely refrozen. [ice crystals -> refreeze]\nF31 Restock everything used during the outage within one week, starting with water and batteries. [restock within 1 week; start water/batteries]\nF32 Finally, write a one-page note about what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one of those gaps before the next outage arrives. [one-page note; fix >=1 gap]\nTotal checkable facts: 32 (F1..F32).\n\n=== STEP 2 - CANONICAL SERIALIZATION + HASH ===\nSerialization rule: JSON, keys sorted recursively (ASCII ascending) at every object level, no whitespace, UTF-8. The proposal artifact (verbatim from the Gap's prompt) was JSON.parse'd and re-emitted in this canonical form; the EXACT bytes were persisted (not reconstructed).\nPersisted file: examples/field-guide/runs/r2/r2.1/p2-restructure-lists-and-timing-table/proposal_canon.json\nByte length: 5163. Top-level keys in sorted order: compressedText, expectedMetric, hardConstraintNote, killedLeverCitations ([]), lens, leverId, rationale, title.\nHash command (run in the run dir):\n  cd runs/r2/r2.1/p2-restructure-lists-and-timing-table && sha256sum proposal_canon.json\nOutput:\n  80126a021fa91c85d65ea986148ab47f7fd276c73ed4aafe2a8fda874353d437 *proposal_canon.json\nseedHex = 80126a021fa91c85d65ea986148ab47f7fd276c73ed4aafe2a8fda874353d437\nVerify: sha256sum of the saved file EQUALS seedHex. PASS. (Fiat-Shamir: proposer never saw this seed.)\n\n=== STEP 3 - DRAW (8 distinct fact indices, deterministic, without replacement) ===\nRule: remaining = [F1..F32] in order (32 facts). Read digest bytes left-to-right as unsigned 8-bit ints b0,b1,... For draw k: n = length(remaining); idx = b_k mod n; pick remaining[idx] (0-based); delete it. Repeat k=0..7.\nDigest first 8 bytes: 80 12 6a 02 1f a9 1c 85  => b0=128, b1=18, b2=106, b3=2, b4=31, b5=169, b6=28, b7=133\nSteps:\n  k=0: n=32, 128 mod 32 = 0  -> F1\n  k=1: n=31, 18  mod 31 = 18 -> F20\n  k=2: n=30, 106 mod 30 = 16 -> F18\n  k=3: n=29, 2   mod 29 = 2  -> F4\n  k=4: n=28, 31  mod 28 = 3  -> F6\n  k=5: n=27, 169 mod 27 = 7  -> F11\n  k=6: n=26, 28  mod 26 = 2  -> F5\n  k=7: n=25, 133 mod 25 = 8  -> F14\nDRAWN (in order): F1, F20, F18, F4, F6, F11, F5, F14 - 8 distinct facts.\n\n=== STEP 4 - COMPREHENSION QUESTIONS (expected answers quoted verbatim from the ORIGINAL sentence) ===\n[1] F1\n  Q: What is the maximum length of power outage this guide prepares a household to get through?\n  A (original): \"a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours\"\n[2] F20\n  Q: How many carbon monoxide alarms should be installed, and on which floors?\n  A (original): \"at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep\"\n[3] F18\n  Q: Where should a headlamp and a lantern each be kept?\n  A (original): \"one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen\"\n[4] F4\n  Q: How much water should be stored per person per day, and what does that total per person for the full seventy-two hours?\n  A (original): \"four litres of water per person per day, which means twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours\"\n[5] F6\n  Q: In what containers should water be stored, where kept, and how often replaced?\n  A (original): \"food-grade containers, keep the containers out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months\"\n[6] F11\n  Q: In what order should food be eaten during the outage?\n  A (original): \"the perishable food from the refrigerator first, then the food from the freezer, and only then open the emergency shelf\"\n[7] F5\n  Q: How is the daily water allowance divided between uses?\n  A (original): \"Half of that allowance is for drinking, and the other half covers cooking and basic hygiene\"\n[8] F14\n  Q: What two things should be done with the refrigerator and freezer doors?\n  A (original): \"Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a note to each door as a reminder\"\n\nRe-derivable by any third party: sha256sum proposal_canon.json -> seedHex -> first 8 bytes -> draw rule above -> the same 8 facts and questions."
